+++ b/docs/mod-loading/phase0-baseline-readiness.md
@@ -0,0 +1,65 @@
+# Phase 0 baseline and readiness report
+
+**Status: BLOCKED.** This is a source/build/provenance audit at `a7847eae82aaac32175c9a7d199673d5c9323bcd` on `work/instant-mod-loading`. No Windows build, loaded-DLL capture, hardware inventory, ZZMI run, fixture run, or performance measurement was performed in this environment. Source inspection is not bottleneck attribution and no acceptance gate has passed.
+
+## Build and artifact identity
+
+`StereovisionHacks.sln` is a Visual Studio 2022 solution (file metadata says VS 17.10). `DirectX11.vcxproj` targets a dynamic library, uses MSVC toolset `v143`, Windows SDK target `10.0`, and defines x64 `Zip Release`. Its output is named `d3d11.dll` under `x64\Zip Release\`. It links checked-in x64 Nektra hook and PCRE2 static libraries, Windows SDK libraries, and solution project references (`BinaryDecompiler`, `D3D_Shaders`, `DirectXTK`). The corresponding Visual Studio installation needs the C++ desktop workload, MSVC v143 x64/x86 build tools, and a Windows 10 SDK; use the actual checked-in dependency files rather than substituting library versions. This is source-derived readiness, not a successful build claim.
+
+Run from a clean worktree on Windows in a VS 2022 Developer PowerShell:
+
+```powershell
+$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
+$rev = git rev-parse HEAD
+if ($rev -ne 'a7847eae82aaac32175c9a7d199673d5c9323bcd') { throw "Unexpected source revision: $rev" }
+msbuild .\StereovisionHacks.sln /m /p:Configuration='Zip Release' /p:Platform=x64 /v:minimal
+$dll = (Resolve-Path '.\x64\Zip Release\d3d11.dll').Path
+Get-FileHash -LiteralPath $dll -Algorithm SHA256
+(Get-Item -LiteralPath $dll).VersionInfo | Format-List FileVersion,ProductVersion,OriginalFilename
+```
+
+The command is **documented but untested**. Keep the build log, tool versions (`msbuild -version`, `cl`), source revision, DLL SHA-256, and file version with the run record. Hash, not the often-generic PE version resource, is the definitive binary identity.
+
+After deploying the candidate through the normal isolated ZZMI test setup and starting the game, verify the DLL actually mapped into the game process. Replace the process name below with the observed game executable (elevate PowerShell if module enumeration is denied):
+
+```powershell
+$game = Get-Process -Name '<game-process-name>' | Select-Object -First 1
+$module = $game.Modules | Where-Object ModuleName -IEQ 'd3d11.dll'
+$module | Select-Object ModuleName,FileName
+Get-FileHash -LiteralPath $module.FileName -Algorithm SHA256
+(Get-Item -LiteralPath $module.FileName).VersionInfo | Format-List FileVersion,ProductVersion,OriginalFilename
+```
+
+Record the process ID and full mapped path. Compare the mapped-file SHA-256 with the just-built DLL hash; a matching filename alone proves nothing. Capture the active `d3dx.ini`, mod/profile identity, game build, importer/ZZMI version, and whether another wrapper or system `d3d11.dll` won module resolution.
+
+## Source audit and attribution boundary
+
+At the pinned tree, useful audit entrypoints are:
+
+- `DirectX11/CommandList.cpp`: `CustomResource::Substantiate()` (around 6407) is deferred until the actual device and binding flags are known; it enters resource-creation mode and dispatches file loading or resource creation. `LoadBufferFromFile()` (around 6471) calls `CreateFile`, `GetFileSize`, `malloc`, and `ReadFile` synchronously before buffer creation. Follow `LoadFromFile` and texture substantiation paths as well; this sample does not quantify call frequency or time.
+- `DirectX11/CommandList.cpp`: `CustomShader::substantiate()` (around 2613), called by `RunCustomShaderCommand::run()` (around 2819), creates shader and related state lazily. Audit this separately from shader replacement/deferred processing in `HackerContext.cpp` (`DeferredShaderReplacement*`).
+- `DirectX11/HookedDevice.cpp`: device hooks include SRV/UAV/RTV/DSV view creation. Trace `Create*View` callers and `CommandList.cpp` `CreateCompatibleView` (around 11902) / resource-view caches. Count resource and view creation independently.
+- `DirectX11/HackerDXGI.cpp`: `HackerSwapChain::Present` (around 192 onward) is the recurring frame boundary; it dispatches input, updates transitions, executes pending `ReloadConfig`, handles delayed initial reload, then draws overlay and calls the original Present. Instrument each stage and first correct rendered frame; do not label Present duration alone as asset load time.
+- `DirectX11/input.cpp`: `DispatchInputEvents`/key binding callbacks are the input path. Reload is deferred using `G->gReloadConfigPending`; trace request time, dispatch, reload start/end, and resulting visible frame.
+- `DirectX11/IniHandler.cpp`: `ParseResourceSections()` (around 2179) clears deferred bind flags, `customResourcePools`, and `customResources` before repopulating. `ReloadConfig()` (around 5159) holds `G->mCriticalSection` while clearing bindings/profiling state, saving persistent state, parsing INIs/includes, optimizing command lists, and deferring shader processing. It then resizes/binds INI resources and runs `InitIniParams()` via the context. Measure lock hold/wait and these subphases separately. Source confirms wide synchronization scope and destructive map rebuild; it does not establish that reload caused the observed activation delay.
+- Context/device lifecycle: `HackerContextFactory` and `HackerContext::~HackerContext()` in `HackerContext.cpp`, `HackerDevice::SetHackerContext()` in `HackerDevice.cpp`, device creation hooks in `HookedDevice.cpp`/`D3D11Wrapper.cpp`, and swapchain setup in `HookedDXGI.cpp`. Trace all device/context create, replacement, and teardown paths; associate all prepared device objects with that device lifetime. Confirm the actual ZZMI device and creation flags at runtime.
+
+Track separately: source/file reads and bytes, decode, shader compile/replacement, resource creation, view creation, command-list/config work, lock wait/hold, input dispatch, any readiness wait, activation request, and first correct complete frame. Include cache hit/miss, CPU/RAM/VRAM use, and frame-time p50/p95/p99/max. Images or captures must demonstrate correct output, since a fast but incomplete replacement is not a pass.
+
+## Repeatable paired ZZMI benchmark
+
+Use one fixed ZZMI/game build, profile, mod package, scene, character, outfit, camera, graphics settings, and storage setup. Record importer/game/DLL hashes, GPU/driver, CPU, RAM, VRAM budget, storage, and active config. Choose and document a known-correct mod with a repeatable visible replacement and a fixed save/route that reproduces the same scene. Use frame capture/video or an agreed frame marker to timestamp request and first complete correct appearance. Keep instrumentation identical between baseline and candidate and export traces off the render path.
+
+For each scenario, run matched baseline/candidate pairs from the same initial state, alternating order (AB/BA) to reduce order and thermal bias; restart the game between cold-start trials and do not silently clear or reuse caches. Use at least five pairs for initial cold-start investigation and at least 100 warm activations per qualified scenario for the plan’s initial p99 estimate; state uncertainty and sample size, record every trial, and report distributions rather than only the best run. Keep cold first use distinct from warm repeats. Run: (1) first appearance after load, (2) character revisit, (3) every available outfit toggle, (4) observed LOD transitions, (5) F10/config refresh with no file change, and (6) newly added mod. During preparation/gameplay capture frame-time traces and long stalls. Verify the final image/state for each pair. A setup-specific count of pairs is a measurement protocol, not a claim of statistical sufficiency for release.
+
+Separate preparation latency from request-to-visible activation latency. Preserve raw traces, run notes, and screenshots/video. Attribute a delay only when stage instrumentation explains it; otherwise label it unresolved. Report cache state, cold misses, coverage, reads/bytes, decode/compile/create counts, waits, and any delayed appearance without a visible freeze.
+
+## Evidence checklist and Phase 0 exit
+
+- [ ] Pinned source revision and clean worktree recorded; VS/MSVC/SDK versions and successful x64 `Zip Release` build log attached.
+- [ ] Built DLL hash/version and process-mapped DLL path/hash agree; active ZZMI config/profile/game/importer and device identity recorded.
+- [ ] Hardware, driver, RAM/VRAM budgets, and storage characteristics recorded.
+- [ ] Baseline paired trials and raw traces cover all six scenarios; known-correct rendering captures accompany each.
+- [ ] Per-stage timing and resource/view/shader counts support attribution, or each remaining source of delay is explicitly unresolved.
+
+**Exit gate remains blocked** until the Windows build and real game data above exist. This report provides audit leads and a repeatable collection procedure only; it contains no measured baseline, no verified artifact, and no gate verdict.
diff --git a/docs/mod-loading/prepared-loading.md b/docs/mod-loading/prepared-loading.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..a5f4a964b4a32216f7dac6868cab0696183b18ef
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/mod-loading/prepared-loading.md
@@ -0,0 +1,161 @@
+# Experimental prepared resource loading
+
+This branch implements an opt-in native preparation path on the pinned source
+at a7847eae82aaac32175c9a7d199673d5c9323bcd. The user waived the Phase 0
+measurement prerequisite. Existing instrumentation is retained; no game
+performance or rendering acceptance result is implied by that waiver.
+
+## Implemented paths
+
+* Bounded immutable source snapshots, SHA-256 content identity, worker-side
+  lossless decoding for supported WIC layouts, source and decoded cache limits,
+  priorities, cancellation and a reusable worker pool.
+* The real custom-buffer, DDS and WIC loading paths consume prepared memory.
+  Buffers get private mutable copies before legacy resize/override processing.
+* Qualified read-only resources can be prepared on the actual render device
+  during an explicitly declared loading window. GPU identity includes device
+  epoch, source content and effective creation descriptors/options.
+* Production custom shaders use complete six-stage shader/fixed-state bundles;
+  supported full Texture2D SRVs are warmed and reused by exact descriptor and
+  resource identity. Unsupported merge/dynamic state stays on the legacy path.
+* Adapter budget admission, old/new live-object estimates, inactive eviction,
+  active leases, bounded queues and generation fences. Estimates do not prove
+  physical residency or driver memory retirement.
+* Dependency-filtered Windows notifications build candidates in the background.
+  Current include discovery covers added and removed INIs, overflow and explicit
+  rescans. Preparation preserves the explicit activation workflow.
+* Detached production parsing/linking into generation-owned resources, shaders,
+  variables, overrides and command lists. Readers retain the complete generation
+  across draw and Present pre/post commands. Publication requires quiescence,
+  current source/device versions and bounded latest-persistent-state migration.
+* Parsed-file IR reuse with budget-domain identity and deterministic clean-build
+  comparisons. Content-identified shader caching seals actual compiler includes,
+  flags and compiler DLL identity; failed preferred lookups are dependencies too.
+* CPU admission covers source/IR/graph metadata, numeric pool/parameter expansion,
+  buffer padding and encoded texture staging. Driver and codec internals remain
+  conservative estimates, not a guarantee of total process memory use.
+
+## Configuration
+
+The default is off. These settings belong in the importer's existing d3dx.ini,
+not in mod files:
+
+    [ModLoading]
+    mode = off
+    cpu_cache_enabled = 0
+    gpu_cache_enabled = 0
+    adaptive_learning = 0
+    transactional_activation = 0
+    general_fast_refresh = 0
+    cpu_cache_mb = 512
+    gpu_cache_mb = 256
+    disk_cache_mb = 0
+    max_asset_mb = 64
+    worker_threads = 2
+    max_pending_jobs = 64
+    create_budget_ms = 2
+    upload_budget_mb = 16
+    preparation_loading_window = 0
+    auto_activate = 0
+    refresh_key =
+    full_rescan_key =
+    profile_members =
+    profile_name =
+
+Modes are off, adaptive and prepared_profile. Prepared profile conservatively
+requires every declared resource/shader and an audited command closure to be
+supported, CPU/GPU ready, current and pinned before reporting Ready. Unknown
+operations count as compatibility; a useful prepared hit does not imply that
+the complete profile is ready. Adaptive never reports Ready. CPU cache zero
+disables CPU and dependent GPU preparation; GPU cache zero keeps CPU preparation.
+New launcher settings default each feature off. Existing configurations that
+explicitly selected a v1 mode retain their absent CPU/GPU flag behavior. Exact
+Resource/CustomShader IDs in `profile_members` are separated by `|`; empty selects
+all. Observed usage and co-use affect preparation priority, not drawing semantics.
+
+An opt-in background publisher writes bounded scalar diagnostics to
+`.xxmi-preparation/status.json` beside the actual DLL. It includes actual module
+SHA256, PID, timestamp, mode, state and counts. Invalidation tickets prevent old
+readiness updates from replacing newer source/device events. A report can lag
+one writer interval; it is diagnostic and never authorizes activation. Launcher
+reads require authenticated capability metadata, matching DLL hash, fresh data,
+consistent counts and a live reported PID. Status is sampled when settings open
+or Diagnostics is clicked; it is not a continuously refreshed progress display.
+
+GPU creation is admitted only when preparation_loading_window is explicitly
+enabled. Present is not automatically considered a loading screen. Driver calls
+cannot be preempted by the advisory time budget. SINGLETHREADED devices keep
+legacy GPU creation; their CPU cache remains usable.
+
+## Compatibility and remaining work
+
+Unsupported or unproven resource dependencies, mutable outputs, aliases and
+pools retain legacy behavior. Source-only image snapshots may still need decode;
+oversized assets, unknown layouts and device creation failures remain misses.
+Sharing prepared GPU resources requires a supported static command closure.
+Unproven writable pipeline aliases keep private legacy GPU objects while still
+allowing immutable CPU-byte preparation.
+Aliased files, reparse points, multiply linked files and unwatchable path forms
+cannot enter readiness; lexical directory watches cannot prove them current.
+Paths with distinct Windows 8.3 aliases, including aliases of ancestor folders,
+also use compatibility behavior: notifications may report either name. This is
+a conservative limitation on volumes with short-name creation enabled.
+Shader creation supports all six stages. Windows fixtures exercise all six
+nonnull stages, an explicit null vertex stage and all four fixed-state types.
+Creation coverage does not establish shader rendering correctness in a game.
+
+An optional `refresh_key` applies an already prepared, validated filename refresh
+at a render boundary. The initial supported edit changes exactly one existing,
+uniquely identified static vertex/index buffer's simple ASCII `.buf` filename.
+All remaining configuration bytes, source order, namespaces and recursive
+directory membership must remain unchanged. Runtime admits only a proven direct
+fixed-slot draw usage; pools, indirect access, writes and unknown commands use
+F10. A key press while preparation is incomplete never authorizes later activation.
+Readers pin the complete overlay across all pre/post and shader-stage lists;
+failure preserves the last successful overlay. F10 retains upstream parsing,
+persistence, initialization and reset semantics and clears the overlay explicitly.
+
+`general_fast_refresh` requires `transactional_activation`. Its worker discovers
+and links the current complete configuration, verifies dependencies after arming
+watches, and prepares the candidate on the actual device. `refresh_key` requests
+publication only when readiness is proven at that boundary. Persistent variables
+take their latest values; bounded pure initializers recompute dependent values.
+Unsupported initialization, mutable resources, pools, shader-regex changes and
+unqualified dynamic expressions retain the last-good generation and report a
+compatibility reason. F10 remains the explicit full legacy reload/reset route.
+The selected profile never authorizes a partial configuration publication.
+
+The general pipeline is implemented but still requires Windows integration and
+real-game qualification. Prepared readiness is an internal proof boundary, not
+evidence of first-correct-frame timing or visual correctness. Incremental parsing
+reuses per-file IR; semantic linking rebuilds the complete graph deterministically.
+The optional `disk_cache_mb` limit enables a persistent decoded-pixel cache at
+`.xxmi-preparation/cache` beside the DLL. Zero disables it. Every hit still
+requires a fresh verified source SHA; version, bounds and checksum failures are
+misses. Workers restore pixels within existing CPU reservations, publish entries
+atomically, recover orphan temporary files and enforce disk quota/LRU limits.
+Original source bytes are untouched. This cache avoids repeat image decoding;
+it does not avoid source validation reads. Shader bytecode has a separate bounded,
+checksummed cache with sealed include closure; device objects are never persisted.
+
+Notifications, overflow recovery and the bounded full-rescan action reverify the
+source closure. Noncooperating multi-file writers do not provide an atomic
+installation boundary; a verified consistent candidate is required before commit.
+
+## Build and verification
+
+Use VS2022 v143 C++ tools and a Windows 10 SDK. Build in an isolated directory
+with all deployment/copy build events disabled. Do not invoke CopyToGames.bat.
+The development launcher repository contains the build script, exact source
+patch and CI workflow. It emits unsigned capability metadata bound to the built
+DLL hash; only the existing authorized package signer can enable the launcher's
+capability gate. No trust override or automatic DLL deployment is added.
+
+Portable tests cover CPU snapshots/cache/scheduling, change reconciliation,
+generation ownership, GPU admission policy and memory PNG parsing. Windows
+fixtures additionally exercise WIC, actual notifications and offscreen WARP
+resources, production parser/runtime test exports in a separate fixture DLL,
+standard shader includes and continuously drained tracing. Test exports are
+excluded from the production DLL. These are synthetic tests, not actual-game
+acceptance evidence. The launcher's offline evidence gate records real-game,
+independent visual/timing review and authorized signing as separate requirements.
